00:02:31.735 --> 00:02:35.650
Microservices seem to be the
rage in software engineering

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.450
today, and rightly so.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:45.540
Traditionally, monolithic
applications were the norm.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.820
And with monolithic
applications,

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.470
the problem is that the codebase
becomes bloated over time.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.100
It can be difficult to determine
where code needs to be changed,

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.060
and packages can have
tangled dependencies.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.660
The entire application needs
to be deployed and tested

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.440
even if a change is made to a
small part of the application.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.430
This increases the
effort and the risk

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.070
that you have to take when
you're making feature changes

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:14.750
and bug fixes.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.520
Microservices, on
the other hand,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.460
enable you to structure
your application components

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.710
to match business boundaries.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.580
The code base for each
service is modular.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.020
Each service can be updated
and deployed independently

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.350
without requiring your customers
to change at the same time.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.410
Each service can be
scaled independently

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.120
depending on load.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.050
For each microservice, teams
can use different programming

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.910
languages and tool kits that
work best for the problem

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:50.160
that they are trying to solve.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:59.680
Well-designed microservices
are loosely coupled at runtime.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.070
Tightly coupled components
can make an application

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:07.060
less resilient to failures,
spikes in traffic, and changes

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.560
to services.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.010
And intermediate components
such as a message queue

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.900
can be used to implement
loose coupling,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.370
perform asynchronous
processing, and buffer messages

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.500
in case of spikes in traffic.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.700
You can use a Cloud Pub/Sub
topic as a message queue.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.850
Publishers can publish
messages to the topic

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.810
and subscribers can subscribe
to messages from the topic.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.280
In the example
that you see here,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.660
the order service can
continue to publish messages

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:41.710
to the message queue even if
the inventory service is down.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.660
And later when the inventory
service comes back online,

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.400
it can retrieve all orders
from the message queue,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.190
including the ones that came
in when the service was down.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:53.530
That's pretty cool.

