00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:14.080
So you're probably sold on
the benefits of microservices.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.670
You've been listening to the
buzz in the industry today.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.070
Microservices have
many advantages,

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.860
but they bring a new set
of challenges related

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:27.910
to complexity, communication
between services, latency,

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:29.290
and debugging.

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:32.170
Debugging, logging, and
monitoring a distributed

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.030
application can seem
pretty daunting.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:42.310
Stackdriver helps provide
greater visibility

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:45.100
and increase
application reliability.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.880
You can monitor GCP and
multicloud environments,

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.570
identify trends
and prevent issues,

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:57.040
reduce monitoring overhead,
and fix problems faster.

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.360
Stackdriver improves
signal-to-noise ratio.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:04.370
Remember, you want to wake
up your on-call support folks

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.110
for real emergencies only.

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:12.370
You really don't want
to be in a situation

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:15.400
where you find out from your
customers on social media

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:17.600
that your application is down.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:20.470
It's important to monitor the
status of your application

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.440
and services to ensure
that they are always

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.630
available and
performing optimally.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:29.920
The monitoring data can be
used to automatically alert

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:33.790
operations teams as soon as
the system begins to fail.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:37.300
Operations teams can then
diagnose and address the issue

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:40.520
promptly, ideally before
your customers even realize

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:41.760
that the application is down.

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:49.010
Implement a health-check
endpoint for each service.

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:51.380
The [INAUDIBLE] handler
should check the health

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:54.680
of all dependencies
and infrastructure

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:58.980
components required for the
service to function properly.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:02.230
For example, the endpoint
handler that you see here

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.640
can check the performance
and availability

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.670
of storage, database,
and network connections

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:10.290
required by the service.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:13.750
The endpoint handler should
return an HTTP response

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:16.770
code of 200 for
successful health checks.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:20.120
And if the health check
fails, the endpoint handler

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:25.820
can return a response code of
503 or a more specific response

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:28.210
code, depending on the error.

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:31.215
A health-monitoring agent
such as a load balancer

00:11:31.215 --> 00:11:36.200
or Stackdriver Monitoring can
periodically send requests

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:38.030
to a health-check endpoint.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.750
Load balancers use health checks
to determine if an application

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:45.050
instance is healthy and is
capable of receiving traffic.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.810
If the health check fails
beyond the threshold

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:50.690
that you have defined,
Stackdriver Monitoring

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.202
can send you an
alert automatically.

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.070
Treat your logs
as event streams.

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:00.140
That's another
best practice here.

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:02.600
Logs constitute a
continuous stream

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.570
of events that keep occurring
as long as your application is

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:07.290
running.

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:10.060
Don't manage log files
in your application.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.380
That can get really
clunky really fast.

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:15.320
Instead, write to an event
stream such as standard

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:18.710
out and let the underlying
infrastructure collate

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:22.370
all events for later
analysis and storage.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:25.730
With this approach, you can
set up logs-based metrics

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:30.380
and trace requests across
services in your application.

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:33.510
With Google Stackdriver, you
can debug your application,

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:37.700
set up error reporting, set up
logging and logs-based metrics,

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:40.460
trace requests across
services, and monitor

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.437
applications running in
a multicloud environment.

