This application has a
health-check endpoint

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:48.620
implemented, and you
can see the screen here

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:53.060
that Stackdriver is
doing a health check

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:57.200
every couple of minutes or
so and receiving data back.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:57.980
Pretty neat.

00:14:57.980 --> 00:14:59.480
MYLENE BIDDLE: Pretty cool.

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:01.430
So I'm going to switch
to the trace list.

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:07.930
And so here in the trace
list we have some information

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:09.130
about the spans.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:11.710
So each span
represents, what are

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:14.170
the steps that was completed
during the processing

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:14.916
of a request?

00:15:14.916 --> 00:15:16.540
So I'm going to filter
it because there

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.360
is a lot of information,
and I'm just

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:22.890
going to look at the orders.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:25.550
And if you recall, we
made a couple of orders.

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:27.170
If we click here
on the span, we're

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:29.570
going to get more
information about what

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:31.790
was happening in each step.

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:33.890
If we scroll down and
look at the timeline,

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:37.160
we get more information
about the steps

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.860
of the requests that are
happening both in parallel

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:40.947
and sequentially.

00:15:40.947 --> 00:15:42.780
SOWMYA KANNAN: So that's
really nice, right?

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:45.920
So you can visualize
which of your calls

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:49.040
are happening in a
serial manner and which

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:51.050
are happening in parallel
and even identify

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:52.610
areas of optimization.

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:55.350
So if you see that there's
a bunch of calls happening

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:58.740
serially which you could
instead do in parallel,

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.800
then that would reduce
the amount of time

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:03.860
that it takes for you
to complete request.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:07.280
So really great
insight into what's

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:09.574
happening under the hood
with your application.

00:16:09.574 --> 00:16:11.240
MYLENE BIDDLE: Yeah,
it really gives you

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:14.030
a good overall
picture from a lot

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:15.680
of data points of
what's happening

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:20.310
in a timeline-based view
in your application.

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:22.640
So now that we've looked a
little bit into the trace,

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:25.710
I'm going to go over to
Stackdriver Error Reporting

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.430
and look into that error that
we tried to replicate that,

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.490
let's say, a customer
told us they encountered.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:33.230
So when we navigate
to Error Reporting,

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.660
the first thing we
see is an error.

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:38.630
And for transparency,
I did generate

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:41.420
the error a couple of times
before we started this demo,

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:43.880
but we did generate
it once here.

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:46.370
So when we click into
the error details,

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:48.570
it's going to show you how
many times this happened.

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:49.910
So obviously if you
were replicating

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:51.910
something a customer saw
you could say, OK, well

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:52.880
I did it once.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.380
The customer saw it 15 times.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:55.610
We need to fix this.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:57.600
It tells you the
first time it was seen

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:00.000
and then the last time it
was seen, super helpful.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.600
So this is the one I
generated just now.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.500
We have a couple
more details up here.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:06.829
If you click on
the Overflow menu,

00:17:06.829 --> 00:17:10.119
it gives you the option to turn
on notifications for the error

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:10.828
group.

00:17:10.828 --> 00:17:12.619
That's really useful
so that you can always

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:15.589
monitor what's going on,
and it's one click away.

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:19.339
You also have a
resolution status.

00:17:19.339 --> 00:17:21.319
With the resolution
status you can go in here

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:23.930
and for this error group you
can say, I've acknowledged it.

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:25.040
Our team's working on it.

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:26.339
You can say it's resolved.

00:17:26.339 --> 00:17:30.410
If you resolve an error group
and then it crops up again,

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:32.930
you didn't really fix it,
then you will automatically

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:35.690
be notified that the error
group you previously resolved

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:38.210
has now been reopened.

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:41.510
So now that we have that, we
have a couple more details.

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:43.670
If you look down
here in the errors

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.100
and you want to see
exactly what was happening

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:50.240
at the time of the error, I
can click here on View Logs,

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.900
and it's going to navigate
me to Stackdriver Logging.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:56.510
And it's going to
filter down to the time

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:58.740
when that error happened.

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:00.350
A lot of things are
happening though,

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:02.670
so this isn't super
easy to parse through.

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:06.580
So I'm going to filter to
just the errors, because it's

00:18:06.580 --> 00:18:08.290
showing me all of the
logs of everything

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:10.480
that happened for all
of the microservices.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:14.380
And as we saw, there's
a handful, two handfuls.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:15.700
So here is our error.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:18.910
If I expand it and then
I look at the labels,

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:21.070
it gives me lots of
information about the error.

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:23.710
And it's actually telling me
that the error happened right

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.740
here in this front-end pod.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:30.690
If I click here and I look
at Show Matching Entries,

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:34.150
it's then going to go to the
advanced filter for logging

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:36.280
where it's showing me
all of the errors that

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:38.650
happened for that specific pod.

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:40.990
Now I also want to
see what happened

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:44.020
before and after the
error for this pod.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:47.656
And since this detailed
filter has already

00:18:47.656 --> 00:18:49.030
been filled out
for me, I'm going

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:54.760
to remove the severity of
error, resubmit the filter.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:57.160
And now I can see,
here's my error.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:00.670
Here is what happened exactly
before the error and then

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:02.374
what happened after it.

00:19:02.374 --> 00:19:03.790
SOWMYA KANNAN:
That's pretty cool.

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:06.810
So you don't have to
wade through log files.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:11.170
Instead, you're able to quickly
zone in on the problem area

00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:14.587
and try to understand what's
going on with your application.

00:19:14.587 --> 00:19:15.670
MYLENE BIDDLE: Absolutely.

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:18.380
It's very powerful
to do it this way.

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:21.470
So now that we have some
context around what's wrong,

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:22.710
let's go back to that error.

00:19:29.759 --> 00:19:32.300
And so we're going to dive into
the problem a little bit more

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:33.830
by looking at snapshots.

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:37.340
A snapshot captures the
local variables and call

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:40.340
stack at a specific line
location in your source code.

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:42.770
So when we look at
the error details,

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:44.390
you'll see that it
actually tells you

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:48.060
the exact line of code where
this broke, right here.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:51.810
It's in the index.js
file at line 165.

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:55.750
So if I click here, it
is going to redirect me

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:56.890
to Stackdriver Debug.

00:20:01.112 --> 00:20:03.940
We'll wait for that to load.

